nodecode-cloro is a rewrite of cloro-dev's cloro-dev/hermes-plugin-cloro:

https://github.com/cloro-dev/hermes-plugin-cloro
commit 7189fbd9ab14aafaa3fb18f419ca76d46aa1bc4a

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 cloro (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin registered a web-search backend on its harness,
named "cloro", which the harness's own web_search tool called. There is no
backend registry to register into here, so the same request is a tool of this
cell's own, cloro_search, and a Lisp verb, cloro:search, answering the same
text. It POSTs the same two members -- the query and a 2-letter ISO 3166-1
country, upcased, refused when it is not two letters -- to
api.cloro.dev/v1/monitor/google, with the key in the same Authorization:
Bearer header, the same JSON and Accept headers, and the same 120-second
ceiling (cloro opens the live results page while it answers). It lifts the
same fields out of result.organicResults -- link, title, snippet -- skips a
result with no resolved http or https link, and stops after the count the
call asked for (5 when it names none, clamped to 1-20). A failure carries
cloro's own error sentence, as the original's error_message did. The key
moves from CLORO_API_KEY into the cell's `cloro' section, which still reads
that variable when no key is pasted there, so the plugin's
"available only when the secret is set" is here a section that names no key
refusing the start.

What was left out: the harness's provider interface itself -- is_available,
get_setup_schema, the "paid" badge and the dashboard link it carried -- which
has no counterpart here; the section's own guide and the setup panel say the
same things in this harness's words. The original answered a JSON object with
a "data.web" array; this cell answers the same fields as one block a result,
which is what a model reads, and signals CLORO-ERROR instead of
returning an "error" member. Two small additions: the HTML tags and entities
Google sends in a title or a snippet are cleaned off, and the plugin's
dashboard link loses its marketing parameters.
